{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction for assault, theft, drug activity and human trafficking convictions","requirement":"A landlord may seek eviction when the tenant is convicted of, pleads guilty to, or is civilly found liable for assault, threats, theft or drug activity, or human trafficking on the premises; a three-day Notice to Quit and a two-year filing limit apply.","key_value":"3-day notice to quit; 2-year filing limit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants covered by the State eviction law, including tenants who knowingly permit a person convicted of, or civilly liable for, these offenses to reside on the premises.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No eviction suit may be brought more than two years after: the juvenile was found to be delinquent; conviction of the person; or after the person’s release from incarceration, whichever is later. A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least three day s prior to filing a suit for (N.J.S.A. 2A-18-61.1(o)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"A tenant in a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may be evicted only when a court orders it, for holding over, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, damage, rule or lease violations; a three-month or one-month notice to quit applies depending on the tenancy.","key_value":"3-month or 1-month notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings; month-to-month and other tenancies get a one-month notice and at-will or year-to-year tenancies a three-month notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"owner-occupied two- or three-family dwelling"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three (3) month notice to quit must be gi ven if an at will tenancy or year -to-year tenancy exists. A one (1) month notice to quit is required for a month-to-month tenancy and other types of tenancies are entitled to a one (1) month notice to quit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction requirements","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good-cause and notice requirements of the Eviction Law, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"Eviction Law good cause; 3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless a Rooming and Boarding House Act exception applies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for misuse of owner-occupancy, board-up or demolition evictions","requirement":"A landlord who evicts a tenant to personally occupy the unit must occupy it at least six months; a landlord who evicts to board up, demolish or retire a building from residential use may not use it residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use ban; treble damages; up to $10,000 civil penalty","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who gave a tenant notice of intent to personally occupy, permanently board up, demolish or retire the dwelling from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Former tenant may recover three times the damages plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation and no planned real estate development registration for five years.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the  landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal prohibited against tenants exercising civil rights","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, substantially alter a lease or its terms, or refuse to renew when a tenant enforces lease or law rights, complains in writing to a government authority about health or safety violations, or organizes.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes; owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings are excepted.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings excepted"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"Tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant notice and protection in foreclosure","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction; a buyer who will personally occupy the unit must give a 90-day notice to quit, and remains barred until an existing longer lease expires. A new owner must notify tenants within 10 business days of the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10 business days for new-owner notice","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties, including month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal foreclosure law does not preempt any State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy","requirement":"Eligible senior citizens, permanently disabled tenants and certain disabled veterans may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is at or below three times county per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater.","key_value":"40-year protected tenancy; income limit three times county per-person average or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62 before the conversion recording, permanently disabled tenants, or honorably discharged service members 60% or more disabled from service, who lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy","requirement":"The 1992 amendment extends protected tenancy to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible as Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the 1981 Act; at present only Hudson County qualifies.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in buildings converted or being converted in a qualified county; currently only Hudson County qualifies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Hudson County conversions"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 et seq.","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Prohibited rent increase under the Truth in Renting list of unlawful landlord acts","requirement":"A landlord may not increase rent above any rent control or rent leveling ordinance that applies, or, if the property is not subject to rent control, may not impose an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rental properties covered by a local rent control or rent leveling ordinance and rental properties not subject to rent control.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.5,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
